\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Three commutants of the binary tetrahedral group}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{\mathcal O} be the [Hurwitz order](fgap-0016), let #{N} be its
reduced norm and let #{t(x)=x+\bar x}, so
#{x^2-t(x)x+N(x)=0} with #{t(x),N(x)\in\mathbb Z} for
#{x\in\mathcal O}. The Lipschitz order #{L} of that definition has index
#{2} in #{\mathcal O}. Also #{2\mathcal O\subset L}: the generator
#{\omega=(-1+i+j+k)/2} satisfies #{2\omega\in L}. Since #{2} is invertible
modulo #{3}, the inclusion induces an isomorphism #{L/3L\to\mathcal O/3\mathcal O}.
Call the quotient #{A}. This is the first step. It has #{\mathbb F_3}-basis #{1,i,j,k}, with
#{i^2=j^2=-1} and #{ij=-ji=k}.
For a nonzero #{x=a+bi+cj+dk} in a two-sided ideal, the scalar parts of
#{x}, #{ix}, #{jx} and #{kx} are #{a,-b,-c,-d}, so one left multiple
#{y} has nonzero scalar part. Since #{i^2=-1}, the inverse of #{i} is
#{-i}, and #{i(a+bi+cj+dk)i^{-1}=a+bi-cj-dk}. The same rule with #{j}
fixes the scalar part and sends #{i} to #{-i}. Thus
#{y\mapsto\tfrac12(y+iyi^{-1})}, followed by
#{y\mapsto\tfrac12(y+jyj^{-1})}, leaves that nonzero scalar, a unit.
The ideal is all of #{A}, so #{A} is simple.
It is not a division algebra: #{z=1+i+j} has
#{z\bar z=N(z)=3=0}. A minimal left ideal #{S} is therefore proper.
Left multiplication gives a homomorphism #{A\to\operatorname{End}(S)}
whose kernel is a two-sided ideal not containing #{1}, hence zero, and
#{(\dim S)^2\ge 4}. Dimension #{3} is impossible: #{A} would act on the
line #{A/S}, the action is nonzero because #{1} acts as the identity, and
its kernel would be a two-sided ideal of codimension one. Dimension #{4}
would force #{S=A}, which is not a proper ideal. Hence #{\dim S=2} and
#{A\to\operatorname{End}(S)} is an isomorphism by dimension.
This is the second step. Write #{r:\mathcal O\to\operatorname{End}(S)} for the composition.
If #{r(x)} is not scalar, its minimal polynomial has degree #{2}. Comparing
#{r(x)^2-t(x)r(x)+N(x)=0} with the Cayley-Hamilton theorem on #{S} gives
#{\det r(x)=N(x)} in #{\mathbb F_3}, the determinant being the action
on #{\Lambda^2 S}. If #{r(x)=c}, then #{x=c+3w} and
#{N(x)\equiv c^2=\det r(x)}.
This is the third step. The units are the elements of reduced norm #{1}.
The same definition counts them: there are #{24},
and #{r} sends them into #{\operatorname{SL}(S)}. This unit group is the
binary tetrahedral group #{2T}. If #{r(u)=1}, then #{u-1=3w} with
#{w\in\mathcal O}, and #{9N(w)=N(u-1)}. Expanding the norm gives
#{N(u-1)=(u-1)(\bar u-1)=N(u)-t(u)+1=2-t(u)}. Writing #{u=a+v}
with #{a} the scalar part, #{N(u)=a^2+N(v)} and #{t(u)=2a}. Since
#{N(v)} is a sum of three squares, #{a^2\le 1}, so #{t(u)^2\le 4}
and #{2-t(u)\le 4}. Thus #{9N(w)\le 4}. Since #{N(w)} is a nonnegative
integer, #{N(w)=0}. The reduced norm is a sum of four squares, so
#{w=0}. Thus the kernel is trivial.
#{\operatorname{GL}(S)} acts simply transitively on ordered bases, so its
order is #{(3^2-1)(3^2-3)=48}. The determinant is surjective onto
#{\mathbb F_3^{\times}} because scaling one vector of a basis attains
every nonzero value, and #{\lvert\operatorname{SL}(S)\rvert=24}. An
injective homomorphism between groups of order #{24} is an isomorphism
##{2T\cong\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb F_3).}}

\p{Remark. In the basis of #{S} fixed by
##{\iota=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\eta=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\rho=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},}
the images of #{i}, #{j} and #{\omega} are #{\iota}, #{\eta} and #{\rho}.
Set #{\kappa=\iota\eta}. Matrix multiplication gives
#{\iota^2=\eta^2=-I}, #{\iota\eta=-\eta\iota}, #{\rho^3=I}, and
##{\rho\iota\rho^{-1}=\kappa,\qquad
\rho\kappa\rho^{-1}=\eta,\qquad
\rho\eta\rho^{-1}=\iota.}
These displayed relations are a coordinate check of step 3.}

\p{Remark. Over a finite field #{\mathbb F_q}, every central simple algebra
of dimension #{4} is #{\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb F_q}(S)} with
#{\dim S=2}. By the Artin-Wedderburn theorem such an algebra is a matrix
ring over a division algebra finite-dimensional over its center, and a
finite division ring is commutative, so the division algebra equals
#{\mathbb F_q} and the dimension forces #{2\times 2} matrices. The
commutativity is Wedderburn's little theorem. Let #{D} be a finite
division ring with center #{Z}, write #{q=\lvert Z\rvert} and
#{n=[D:Z]}. For #{a\notin Z} the centralizer has order #{q^d-1} with
#{d\mid n} and #{d<n}, and the class equation of #{D^{\times}}, summed over
representatives outside the center, is
##{q^n-1=q-1+\sum\frac{q^n-1}{q^d-1}.}
In #{\mathbb Z[x]}, #{x^k-1=\prod_{e\mid k}\Phi_e(x)}. If #{d\mid n} and
#{d<n}, then #{\Phi_n(x)} divides #{(x^n-1)/(x^d-1)}, because #{n} does
not divide #{d}. Thus #{\Phi_n(q)} divides every term of the sum and
divides #{q^n-1}, so it divides #{q-1}. If #{n>1}, then
#{\Phi_n(q)=\prod(q-\zeta)} over the primitive #{n}th roots of unity.
For each such root other than #{1}, writing #{\zeta=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta}
gives
#{\lvert q-\zeta\rvert^2=(q-\cos\theta)^2+\sin^2\theta>(q-1)^2},
so #{\lvert\Phi_n(q)\rvert>q-1}, which cannot divide #{q-1}. Hence
#{n=1} and #{D} is the field #{Z}. This is the theorem of
J. H. M. Wedderburn, A theorem on finite algebras, Trans. Amer. Math.
Soc. #{6} (1905), pp. 349-352.
The same conclusion removes the choice, over #{\mathbb F_q}, between a
compact and a split quaternion algebra, #{\mathbb H} versus
#{M_2(\mathbb R)} over #{\mathbb R}. What remains in
#{\operatorname{SL}(S)} is a choice of torus. A split torus consists of
the elements acting by #{a} and #{a^{-1}} on a decomposition
#{S=\ell_1\oplus\ell_2}; as #{a} runs through #{\mathbb F_q^{\times}} the
group has order #{q-1}. A non-split torus is the group of norm-one units
of a quadratic extension #{E\cong\mathbb F_{q^2}} inside
#{\operatorname{End}(S)} for which #{S} is one-dimensional over #{E}.
Multiplication by a field element has determinant equal to its field
norm, so the norm-one elements lie in #{\operatorname{SL}(S)}, and the
norm #{E^{\times}\to\mathbb F_q^{\times}} has kernel of order
#{(q^2-1)/(q-1)=q+1}. For #{q=3} the orders are #{2} and #{4}. In
#{2T} they are realized by #{\{\pm 1\}} and by #{\langle i\rangle}:
the first is the scalar group of order #{2}, and #{i^2=-1} gives a root
of #{x^2+1}, which has no root in #{\mathbb F_3}, so
#{\mathbb F_3(i)} is the field of order #{9} and #{\langle i\rangle}
has order #{4}.}

\p{\ref{fdip-0053} records the 7 irreducible characters, of
degrees #{1,1,1,2,2,2,3}, and the squares of the conjugacy
classes. Substitution of that table into the
[Frobenius-Schur indicator](fgap-0021)
#{|2T|^{-1}\sum_g\chi(g^2)} gives the numerators
#{24}, #{0}, #{0}, #{-24}, #{0}, #{0} and #{24} for
#{1}, #{\lambda}, #{\lambda^2}, #{\sigma}, #{\sigma\lambda},
#{\sigma\lambda^2} and #{\tau}. The indicators are
#{1,0,0,-1,0,0,1}. For the sample terms,
#{2+2-12-16=-24} is the numerator of #{\sigma}, and
#{3+3+18=24} is the numerator of #{\tau}.
\ref{fdip-0051} converts an indicator into a commutant.
The trivial representation and the degree-#{3} representation
#{\tau} have indicator #{1} and commutant #{\mathbb R}.
The defining representation #{\sigma} has indicator #{-1} and
commutant #{\mathbb H}. The other 4 irreducible characters
have indicator #{0}, and each conjugate pair has commutant
#{\mathbb C}. Thus each of #{\mathbb R}, #{\mathbb H} and
#{\mathbb C} occurs as #{\operatorname{End}_G(W)} for an
irreducible real representation #{W} of this group, and so
occurs as the scalar ring of a
[right multiplicity module](fdip-00KN)
#{\operatorname{Hom}_G(W,H)}.
The isomorphism is an identification of these finite groups.
It is not a physical symmetry assignment.}
